{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#office-of-enrollment-and-marketing-posted-september-30-2026": {
      "title": "Executive Director of Graduate & Online Enrollment",
      "description": "Office of Enrollment and Marketing | Posted September 30, 2026. Job Summary The Executive Director of Graduate & Online Enrollment provides strategic leadership for all graduate and online student recruitment at Baker University. This role serves as the enrollment leadership counterpart to the Executive Director of Enrollment Management, who leads recruitment for the university's on-campus, traditional student population, with the Executive Director of Graduate & Online Enrollment holding equivalent scope and authority for the university's graduate and online populations. Reporting to the Vice President for Enrollment and Marketing, the Executive Director builds, manages, and develops the Graduate & Online Recruitment team and leads the development and execution of strategic recruitment plans that grow enrollment across graduate and online programs. The Executive Director works closely with Marketing to shape strategic marketing plans tailored to graduate and online audiences, and partners with the Office of Advancement to identify and cultivate strategic partnerships, with employers, school districts, corporations, and community organizations, that create new enrollment pipelines. As a key member of the Enrollment and Marketing leadership team, the Executive Director partners closely with the Executive Director of Enrollment Management to ensure a unified, coordinated enrollment strategy across all student populations. This role requires a collaborative, entrepreneurial leader who can build and lead a high-performing recruitment team, translate market insight into strategy, and work effectively across Marketing and Advancement to grow enrollment in Baker University's graduate and online programs. Job Duties 1. Strategic Enrollment Leadership & Recruitment Strategy With the Vice President, establish annual recruitment goals and enrollment forecasts for graduate and online programs aligned with institutional strategic priorities. Develop and implement comprehensive, multi-channel recruitment strategies to grow graduate and online program enrollment across the Graduate School of Education (GSOE) and the School of Professional and Graduate Studies (SPGS). Monitor recruitment funnel performance including inquiries, applications, admits, and deposits, adjusting strategies to improve conversion outcomes. Oversee the management of prospective student pipelines and ensure consistent, personalized engagement throughout the recruitment process. Ensure recruitment staff utilize the university's Customer Relationship Management (CRM) system to track interactions and manage prospective student communications. Analyze recruitment data and conversion metrics to identify opportunities for improvement and growth. Identify emerging market opportunities and collaborate with academic leadership to align program growth with workforce and community needs. Remain current with national trends in graduate enrollment, online education, and adult learner recruitment. 2. Team Leadership & Management Build, lead, and manage the Graduate & Online Recruitment team, including hiring, onboarding, and staffing decisions as the team grows to meet enrollment goals. Provide day-to-day supervision, strategic direction, and performance management for recruitment staff. Mentor and develop staff through coaching, training, and performance feedback. Establish clear expectations, recruitment goals, and performance standards aligned with institutional enrollment objectives. Foster a culture of collaboration, accountability, and results-driven recruitment across the graduate and online enrollment team. 3. Marketing Strategy & Collaboration Work closely with Marketing to develop and execute a strategic marketing plan tailored to graduate, online, and adult-learner audiences. Partner with Marketing to align messaging, branding, and campaigns with graduate and online enrollment priorities and target audiences. Provide market and prospective-student insight to inform marketing content, channel strategy, and campaign performance. Review recruitment and marketing performance data jointly with Marketing to continuously refine outreach strategy. 4. Strategic Partnerships (in Collaboration with Advancement) Partner with the Office of Advancement to identify, cultivate, and develop strategic partnerships with employers, school districts, corporations, and community organizations that create new graduate and online enrollment pipelines. Provide recruitment and enrollment expertise to support Advancement-led partnership conversations, cohort programs, and employer-sponsored education initiatives. Track and evaluate the enrollment outcomes of partnerships developed in collaboration with Advancement, sharing results with the Vice President. Represent the Enrollment and Marketing office in partnership discussions led by Advancement, as needed. 5. Reporting, Budget & Operations Monitor and analyze enrollment trends including inquiry sources, application flow, program demand, retention, and completion patterns. Prepare regular enrollment reports and updates for the Vice President for Enrollment and Marketing and university leadership. Develop and manage the recruitment team's operating budget. Ensure compliance with university reporting standards and support activities related to accreditation and institutional effectiveness. 6. Collaboration & Campus Partnerships Collaborate closely with Deans, Program Directors, faculty, and academic leadership to support program promotion and enrollment growth. Partner with the Executive Director of Enrollment Management to align messaging, share best practices, and ensure a seamless prospective-student experience across traditional, graduate, and online populations. Serve as a key member of the Enrollment and Marketing leadership team and contribute to the university's broader enrollment strategy. Required Qualifications Master's degree and a minimum of five years of experience in enrollment management, recruitment, admissions, or related fields, including at least two years in a supervisory or team leadership capacity. Demonstrated success building, managing, and developing a high-performing recruitment or sales team. Experience collaborating cross-functionally with marketing, communications, or advancement/development functions. Strong communication, analytical, and relationship-building skills. Ability to analyze data and translate insights into effective recruitment strategy. Ability to travel to recruitment events, conferences, and partner sites as needed. Experience using Customer Relationship Management (CRM) systems and lead tracking tools. Preferred Qualifications Seven or more years of experience in enrollment management, graduate recruitment, or higher education administration. Experience working with adult learners, professional education, or non-traditional student populations. Experience partnering with an advancement, development, or corporate/community relations function. Experience working in a private higher education environment. Demonstrated ability to scale a recruitment team and function to meet growing enrollment goals. To Apply: Submit an online application with a cover letter, resume, and three references at bakeru.edu/application-for-employment or email employment@bakerU.edu ."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#adjunct-faculty-business-posted-november-10-2026": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Business | Posted November 10, 2026",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Business | Posted November 10, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate business courses, such as principles of management, marketing, accounting, or business law, beginning Fall 2027. Qualifications: Master’s degree in business administration or a closely related field (or a master’s with 18 graduate credit hours in the discipline). Teaching experience and relevant professional experience preferred. Adjunct appointments are semester-to-semester, and course availability depends on enrollment. Courses are offered in person on the Baldwin City campus. To apply, send a cover letter, CV, and the names of three references to Kim Lauffer at klauffer@bakeru.edu . Review begins immediately and continues until positions are filled. Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#adjunct-faculty-composition-posted-november-10-2026": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Composition | Posted November 10, 2026",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Composition | Posted November 10, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time adjunct faculty to teach first-year composition and other writing courses, beginning Fall 2027. Courses emphasize thesis-driven writing, research, and revision, with individual conferences. Qualifications: Master’s degree in English, rhetoric and composition, or a closely related field (or a master’s degree in a writing intensive discipline). College-level writing instruction experience preferred. Adjunct appointments are semester-to-semester, and course availability depends on enrollment. Courses are offered in person on the Baldwin City campus. To apply, send a cover letter, CV, and the names of three references to Kim Lauffer at klauffer@bakeru.edu . Review begins immediately and continues until positions are filled. Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#adjunct-faculty-economics-posted-november-10-2026": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Economics | Posted November 10, 2026",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Economics | Posted November 10, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate economics courses, such as principles of micro- and macroeconomics, beginning Fall 2027. Qualifications: Master’s degree in economics or a closely related field (or a master’s with 18 graduate credit hours in economics). Teaching experience preferred. Adjunct appointments are semester-to-semester, and course availability depends on enrollment. Courses are offered in person on the Baldwin City campus. To apply, send a cover letter, CV, and the names of three references to Kim Lauffer at klauffer@bakeru.edu . Review begins immediately and continues until positions are filled. Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#adjunct-faculty-exercise-science-posted-november-10-2026": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Exercise Science | Posted November 10, 2026",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Exercise Science | Posted November 10, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate exercise science courses, such as anatomy and physiology, kinesiology, or exercise physiology, beginning Fall 2027. Lab or practical instruction may be included. Qualifications: Master’s degree in exercise science, kinesiology, or a closely related field (or a master’s with 18 graduate credit hours in the discipline). Teaching experience and relevant certifications (e.g., ACSM, NSCA) preferred. Adjunct appointments are semester-to-semester, and course availability depends on enrollment. Courses are offered in person on the Baldwin City campus. To apply, send a cover letter, CV, and the names of three references to Kim Lauffer at klauffer@bakeru.edu . Review begins immediately and continues until positions are filled. Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#adjunct-faculty-history-posted-november-10-2026": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, History | Posted November 10, 2026",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, History | Posted November 10, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate history courses, such as U.S. history surveys, world history, or Western civilization, beginning Fall 2027. Qualifications: Master’s degree in history or a closely related field (or a master’s with 18 graduate credit hours in history). Teaching experience preferred. Adjunct appointments are semester-to-semester, and course availability depends on enrollment. Courses are offered in person on the Baldwin City campus. To apply, send a cover letter, CV, and the names of three references to Kim Lauffer at klauffer@bakeru.edu . Review begins immediately and continues until positions are filled. Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#adjunct-faculty-sociology-posted-november-10-2026": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty, Sociology | Posted November 10, 2026",
      "description": "Adjunct Faculty, Sociology | Posted November 10, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences seeks part-time adjunct faculty to teach undergraduate sociology courses, such as introduction to sociology, social problems, or research methods, beginning Fall 2027. Qualifications: Master’s degree in sociology or a closely related field (or a master’s with 18 graduate credit hours in sociology). Teaching experience preferred. Adjunct appointments are semester-to-semester, and course availability depends on enrollment. Courses are offered in person on the Baldwin City campus. To apply, send a cover letter, CV, and the names of three references to Kim Lauffer at klauffer@bakeru.edu . Review begins immediately and continues until positions are filled. Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#assistant-associate-full-professor-of-business-and-economics-posted-november-18-2026": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Business and Economics | Posted November 18, 2026",
      "description": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Business and Economics | Posted November 18, 2026. Baker University’s College of Arts and Sciences Business and Economics Department seeks applications for open tenure-track positions to begin August 1, 2027. Along with a passion for undergraduate teaching and an appreciation for the role of the business and economics curriculum in a liberal arts setting, strong candidates will have teaching expertise in two or more of the following areas: analytics, economics, finance, management, international business, entrepreneurship. Strong candidates will also show evidence of successful teaching and effective support and guidance for students pursuing internships and display an eagerness to actively engage with the Kansas City area and wider global business communities in support of our students’ professional aspirations. Experience successfully engaging alumni in mentorship and other roles in support of the student experience is also desired. Opportunities for program leadership also exist, particularly for those more experienced candidates who have previously held leadership roles within their programs or institutions. A master’s level degree in business or a related field is required, along with relevant work experience. A doctoral degree in a business-related discipline is preferred. Applicants should not require sponsorship for employment visa status (e.g. H-1B visa status). Founded by a Methodist bishop in 1858, Baker is the oldest university in Kansas. The Department is part of the University’s College of Arts and Sciences located in Baldwin City, Kansas (residential four-year campus). The Department offers majors in Accounting, Business, Economics, International Business, and Sports Administration. The Accounting, Business and International Business programs are accredited by the Accreditation Council for Business Schools and Programs (ACBSP). The Department is also an important contributor to the data analytics and pre-law interdisciplinary minor programs. Positions may be tenure-track or non-tenure-track and rank will reflect the candidate’s professional and academic experience. The full-time teaching load at Baker’s Baldwin City campus is 4 + 4 (two-semester calendar). Eligible candidates will be considered based on their (1) teaching experience, (2) demonstrated ability to foster student engagement and success, and (3) ongoing professional learning. Courses that these positions will be asked to cover will depend on the candidate’s experience and qualifications, but strong candidates will be well-prepared to teach in at least two of the following areas: Data Analytics (introductory and advanced topics), Economics (introductory and advanced topics), Corporate Finance (introductory and advanced topics), International Management, Investment Theory, International Finance, Strategic Management, Entrepreneurship (introductory and advanced topics), and Ethics in Business. To begin the application process, please complete the “Online Application” form available at www.bakerU.edu/jobs (see link at top of the page). Note that you can skip the “Employment Experience,” “Education,” and “References” sections by uploading documents at the bottom of the form. A complete application will include a cover letter, curriculum.vitae, evidence of teaching effectiveness, statement of teaching philosophy, and names with contact information for three references. Queries regarding the position may be sent to Dr. Kevin Conley, Chair of the Business and Economics Department and Search Committee Chair ( kconley@bakeru.edu ). Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, continuing until all positions are filled."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#executive-director-for-enrollment-posted-november-20-2026": {
      "title": "Executive Director for Enrollment | Posted November 20, 2026",
      "description": "Executive Director for Enrollment | Posted November 20, 2026. Department: Office of Enrollment and Marketing Reports to: Vice President for Enrollment and Marketing Supervises: Undergraduate Admissions Counselors, Graduate and Online Enrollment Counselors, and the Director of Enrollment Engagement About the Role Baker University is seeking an Executive Director for Enrollment to serve as the second-ranking leader of the Office of Enrollment and Marketing and the Vice President's principal partner in developing and carrying out the university's enrollment strategy. This role provides day-to-day leadership for recruitment and admissions across all student populations: traditional undergraduate, graduate, and online. Reporting to the Vice President for Enrollment and Marketing, you will directly supervise the undergraduate admissions counselors and the graduate and online enrollment counselors, and oversee the Director of Enrollment Engagement, who leads admissions events, campus visits, and the ambassador program. You will translate institutional enrollment goals into strategic plans, make sure those plans are executed across the full admissions cycle, and hold the team accountable for achieving enrollment goals. You will work closely with Marketing to shape recruitment campaigns for each audience, partner with the Office of Advancement to build strategic partnerships that create new enrollment pipelines, and collaborate across campus to support a smooth transition from admit to enrolled student. We are looking for a collaborative, entrepreneurial leader who can manage a growing team, use data to drive decisions, and represent the Vice President with campus and external partners. What You Will Do Strategic Enrollment Leadership Serve as the Vice President's strategic partner and second in command, leading implementation of enrollment strategy across undergraduate, graduate, and online programs. With the Vice President, establish annual recruitment goals and enrollment forecasts for each student population, aligned with institutional strategic priorities. Develop and implement comprehensive, multi-channel recruitment strategies, including for the Graduate School of Education (GSOE) and the School of Professional and Graduate Studies (SPGS). Provide regular input on progress toward strategic goals and recommendations for how to reach them. Monitor funnel performance, including inquiries, applications, admits, and deposits, and adjust strategies to improve conversion and yield. Identify emerging market opportunities and work with academic leadership to align program growth with workforce and community needs. Stay current with national trends in undergraduate, graduate, online, and adult learner enrollment. Act on behalf of the Vice President in the Vice President's absence and represent the office to senior leadership as needed. Team Leadership and Management Directly supervise the undergraduate admissions counselors and the graduate and online enrollment counselors, including hiring, onboarding, and staffing decisions as the team grows. Supervise and support the Director of Enrollment Engagement and ensure events, visits, and ambassador efforts align with recruitment strategy. Set clear expectations, recruitment goals, and performance standards for each counselor and team, and manage performance through regular feedback and evaluation. Mentor and develop staff through coaching, training, and professional development. Foster a culture of collaboration, accountability, and results-driven recruitment. Admissions Operations and Compliance Oversee the full admissions cycle for all populations, from outreach through application review, admission decisions, and yield and enrollment efforts. Ensure recruitment staff use the university's CRM to track interactions and manage communications, with consistent, personalized engagement throughout the process. Ensure admissions policies, procedures, and communications comply with institutional, state, and federal regulations, including FERPA. Oversee admissions processes and operational standards and recommend improvements. Marketing Strategy and Collaboration Partner with Marketing and Communications to develop and execute strategic marketing plans for traditional undergraduate, graduate, online, and adult-learner audiences. Align messaging, branding, publications, communication flows, and campaigns with enrollment priorities and target audiences. Provide market and prospective-student insight to inform content, channel strategy, and campaign performance. Review recruitment and marketing performance data jointly with Marketing to continuously refine outreach. Strategic Partnerships and Pipeline Development Build and maintain relationships with high schools, community colleges, community-based organizations, and college counselors to strengthen the applicant pipeline. Partner with the Office of Advancement to develop strategic partnerships with employers, school districts, corporations, and community organizations that create new graduate and online enrollment pipelines. Provide recruitment and enrollment expertise to support partnership conversations, cohort programs, and employer-sponsored education initiatives. Track and evaluate the enrollment outcomes of partnerships and share results with the Vice President. Reporting, Budget, and Operations Analyze enrollment data and admissions metrics, including applications, acceptance rate, yield, demographics, program demand, retention, and completion patterns, to inform strategy. Prepare regular enrollment reports and updates for the Vice President and university leadership. Develop and manage the enrollment operating budget and allocate resources across recruitment travel, events, visitor experience, and team needs. Support accreditation and institutional effectiveness activities and ensure compliance with university reporting standards. Campus Collaboration and Institutional Leadership Collaborate with Financial Aid, Academic Affairs, Student Life, and the Registrar to support a smooth transition from admit to enrolled student. Work closely with deans, program directors, faculty, and academic leadership to support program promotion and enrollment growth. Serve on enrollment and university committees at the request of senior leadership. Represent the university at admissions events, college fairs, conferences, and with prospective students and families as needed. Required Qualifications Master's degree and a minimum of seven years of progressively responsible experience in enrollment management, admissions, or related fields, including at least three years in a supervisory or team leadership capacity. Demonstrated success building, managing, and developing a high-performing recruitment or admissions team. Experience developing and executing enrollment strategies and managing a budget. Experience collaborating cross-functionally with marketing, communications, financial aid, or advancement and development functions. Strong communication, analytical, and relationship-building skills, with the ability to analyze data and translate insights into effective strategy. Experience using CRM systems and lead tracking tools. Ability to travel to recruitment events, conferences, and partner sites as needed. Preferred Qualifications Ten or more years of experience in enrollment management or higher education administration. Experience leading both traditional undergraduate and graduate or online recruitment. Experience working with adult learners, professional education, or non-traditional student populations. Experience partnering with an advancement, development, or corporate and community relations function. Experience working in a private higher education environment. Demonstrated ability to scale a recruitment team and function to meet growing enrollment goals. How to Apply Submit an online application with a cover letter, resume, and three references at bakeru.edu/application-for-employment or email employment@bakerU.edu . Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    },
    "https://www.bakeru.edu/about-baker/careers-baker#associate-director-of-enrollment-communications-november-20-2026": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Enrollment Communications | November 20, 2026",
      "description": "Associate Director of Enrollment Communications | November 20, 2026. Department: Office of Enrollment and Marketing Reports to: Vice President for Enrollment and Marketing Department: Enrollment and Marketing About the Role Baker University is seeking an Associate Director of Enrollment Communications to design and carry out the communication and marketing efforts that move prospective students from first inquiry to enrollment across undergraduate, graduate, and online programs. This role owns the communication flows for all recruitment efforts, serves as Co-Captain of the university's Slate CRM implementation, and partners with Marketing on recruitment materials, digital campaigns, and social media. Reporting to the Vice President for Enrollment and Marketing, you will build one cohesive, university-wide messaging approach across the College of Arts and Sciences, the School of Professional and Graduate Studies, the School of Education, and the School of Nursing, and develop segmented campaigns for specific audiences rather than one-size-fits-all messaging. You will also manage the external vendors that help generate leads and applications, and provide enrollment insight that informs marketing strategy. The ideal candidate is a strategic, organized, and detail-oriented communicator who is comfortable with data and technology, can manage multiple projects and vendor relationships at once, and thrives as part of a unified Enrollment and Marketing team. What You Will Do Communication Flows and Messaging Strategy Design, build, and manage communication flows for all recruitment efforts, from inquiry through application, admission, and yield, for undergraduate, graduate, and online audiences. Expand the communications strategy to cover all schools, creating one cohesive, university-wide messaging approach that meets brand and quality standards. Coordinate, schedule, and track outgoing communications from the admissions office and admissions counselors, and create streamlined, consistent messaging for counselor use. Launch intentional, segmented campaigns, including parent-focused, location-specific, interest-specific, and special population campaigns. Own communication planning for events, visits, and fairs, including confirmations, follow-ups, and pre-event and post-event messages. Partner with Student Life on first-year checklist and orientation messaging. Slate CRM Co-Captain and Data Serve as Slate Co-Captain, with formal ownership of the university's Slate CRM implementation. Build and maintain the workflows, templates, segments, and automations that power recruitment communications. Design and generate reports to track communication performance and enrollment funnel results. Analyze results, identify opportunities for improvement, and share recommendations with university leadership. Train admissions staff to use the CRM and communication tools consistently. Marketing Materials and Digital Campaigns Partner with Marketing to plan and develop print and digital recruitment materials that support enrollment priorities and target audiences. Partner with Marketing to plan, launch, and evaluate digital campaigns across email, paid, and social channels. Build standardized systems with Marketing for printed materials, digital communications, and key dates and deadlines scheduled in advance. Share enrollment data and prospective-student insight to help shape marketing strategy, messaging, and channel decisions. Participate in regular joint planning meetings with Marketing. Day-to-day design, graphic production, and photography are handled by Marketing. Vendor Management Manage the external vendors that help generate leads, inquiries, and applications, including contracts, timelines, deliverables, and performance. Serve as primary contact for Niche and Niche Engage, including monthly insight reviews, video content, and custom audiences and campaigns. Evaluate vendor results against cost and enrollment outcomes, and recommend renewal, expansion, or changes. Coordinate vendor activity with the broader communication flows and marketing plans. Social Media Help develop the enrollment social media plan and carry it out across Baker University's Admissions Instagram, Facebook, and TikTok accounts. Post regularly and strategically to support recruitment campaigns, events, and key enrollment dates. Work with Marketing to raise the quality and consistency of content, including gathering content from students and campus partners. Monitor channel performance and adjust content and timing based on results. Admissions Team Support and Training Work with senior enrollment leadership to formalize a training process for new admissions counselors, covering college fairs, high school visits, scheduling software (Repvisits, Scoir, and Strivescan), and admissions presentations. Provide ongoing support to counselors on messaging and communication tools. As needed, engage with prospective students and families, including admissions presentations, recruitment events, and college fairs. Required Qualifications Bachelor's degree and a minimum of three years of experience in communications, marketing, enrollment marketing, and/or admissions. Experience planning and managing multi-channel communication campaigns. Experience using a CRM database, such as Slate or Salesforce, to build communications, run reports, and analyze results. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Strong organizational, project management, and planning skills. Ability to work with data and translate results into improvements. Ability to work occasional Saturdays (approximately 1 to 3 per year) and some evenings. Preferred Qualifications Experience implementing or administering Slate. Prior experience in higher education enrollment or marketing. Experience managing external vendors or agency partners. Experience managing organizational social media accounts, including TikTok, Instagram, and Facebook. Experience with email marketing automation tools. How to Apply Submit an online application with a cover letter, resume, and three references at bakeru.edu/application-for-employment or email employment@bakerU.edu . Baker University is an equal opportunity employer."
    }
  },
